.TH ONIONFRUITUX 1 "October 2026" "OnionFruitux 1.0.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
onionfruitux \- send this computer's internet through Tor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B onionfruitux
.br
.B onionfruitux
.I tray
.br
.B onionfruitux route add
.I name
[\fB\-\-entry\fR \fIcc\fR]
[\fB\-\-exit\fR \fIcc\fR]
[\fB\-\-bridge\fR \fIkind\fR]
[\fB\-\-strict\fR]
.br
.B onionfruitux route list
.br
.B onionfruitux route use
.I name
.br
.B onionfruitux route remove
.I name
.br
.B onionfruitux connect
.br
.B onionfruitux status
.br
.B onionfruitux new\-circuit
.br
.B onionfruitux disconnect
.br
.B onionfruitux panic\-off
.br
.B onionfruitux reset\-network
.br
.B onionfruitux doctor
.br
.B onionfruitux plan
.br
.B onionfruitux boot enable
.br
.B onionfruitux boot disable
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B OnionFruitux
is a Linux switch that sends apps which follow the system proxy through
the Tor network. Open the window and turn the switch on, or run
.BR onionfruitux\ connect .
Turn the switch off and those apps use the normal network again.
.PP
While the switch is on, OnionFruitux runs Tor as the system user
.B onionfruitux
and sets the system SOCKS proxy to 127.0.0.1 port 9155. On GNOME that is
.B org.gnome.system.proxy .
On KDE that is the
.B "Proxy Settings"
group in
.IR kioslaverc .
New sessions can also read
.B ALL_PROXY
and
.BR SOCKS_PROXY .
.B http_proxy
is not set. Apps that ignore the system proxy, including Transmission,
qBittorrent, and uTorrent, stay on the normal connection. Hosts listed under
Settings, Network, Ignore hosts, stay direct as well.
.PP
Before those settings are changed, OnionFruitux writes them to
.IR ~/.local/state/onionfruitux/saved\-state.json .
Turning the switch off puts that file back, checks that 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8
answer and that a name resolves, and then deletes the file. If the check
fails, the snapshot is restored again. If it still fails, the window shows
what is still set and offers
.BR "Reset network settings to defaults" ,
which sets the proxy mode to none. Quit and SIGTERM restore the snapshot too.
The next start offers to restore a snapshot left behind by a crash or reboot.
.PP
OnionFruitux does not install iptables or nftables rules, does not rewrite
.IR resolv.conf ,
and does not change NetworkManager, ProtonVPN, or Tailscale. An older version
may have left an
.B onionfruitux
nftables table. Turning the switch off, or running
.BR panic\-off ,
deletes that table if it is still installed and restores a DNS snapshot from
that older version. It does not reconfigure a VPN.
.PP
The window stays responsive while the switch is turning on. The switch slides
on and turns orange, and a status line reports each step, including Tor's
bootstrap percent. It turns green only when Tor reports Bootstrapped 100% and
a request through the SOCKS proxy reaches the Tor check API. The same orange
and green states are used on the tray icon. If connecting fails or takes too
long, the switch returns to off, the saved proxy settings are put back, and
Tor is stopped.
.PP
OnionFruitux runs its own Tor. SOCKS is port 9155 and the control port is
9156. Those ports are not 9050 or 9051, which the
.B tor
package uses, and not 5353, which mDNS uses. Installing this package does
not stop or disable Ubuntu's Tor service. If that service is running,
.B onionfruitux doctor
says so and leaves it running. To stop it yourself:
.EX
sudo systemctl disable \-\-now tor.service tor@default.service
.EE
If one of OnionFruitux's ports is already taken, the switch names the
process that holds it. If Tor itself exits, the error includes the last
lines of its log.
.PP
After the switch turns fully on, OnionFruitux opens
.I https://check.torproject.org/
in the desktop user's browser. The page opens only after Tor is connected
and the system proxy is set. It is not opened by the root process that starts
Tor, and the program does not wait for the browser. Turning the switch off
opens the page again after the saved proxy settings are restored. Turn this
off under Settings, Network, with
.B "Open the Tor check page when the switch changes" .
The boot service does not open a browser.
.PP
The first time the switch is turned on, the desktop asks for an administrator
password so OnionFruitux can start Tor. The proxy is changed as the desktop
user. Polkit remembers that password for a little while. The window is not
frozen while that dialog is open.
.PP
OnionFruitux is an independent project by Nitrofire Computing. It is not
affiliated with or endorsed by The Tor Project or DragonFruit Network.
.SH COMMANDS
With no arguments,
.B onionfruitux
opens the window. The window has the switch,
.BR "New circuit" ,
and
.BR Settings .
.TP
.B tray
Show a panel or dock icon with the same switch. The menu can turn the switch
on or off, ask for a new circuit, or open the window.
.TP
.B route add \fIname\fR
Save a named route. A later
.B route add
with the same name replaces that route.
.B \-\-entry
and
.B \-\-exit
take a two\-letter country code.
.B \-\-bridge
is one of
.BR plain ,
.BR obfs4 ,
.BR snowflake ,
.BR meek ,
.BR webtunnel ,
or
.BR conjure .
A bridge replaces the entry country.
.B \-\-strict
means only use the countries on the route.
.TP
.B route list
List saved routes. The active route is marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.B route use \fIname\fR
Choose the route the next connection will use. If the switch is already on,
turn it off and on to use the new route.
.TP
.B route remove \fIname\fR
Delete a saved route.
.TP
.B connect
Turn the switch on. This starts Tor, waits until Tor is fully connected, saves
the current proxy settings, points the system SOCKS proxy at Tor, and checks
the Tor Project API through that proxy. On failure the saved settings are put
back and Tor is stopped.
.TP
.B disconnect
Turn the switch off, restore the saved proxy settings, and check that the
computer can reach the internet directly.
.TP
.B panic\-off
Stop OnionFruitux's Tor immediately, even if the window froze or the saved
state says the switch is already off. This does not open a browser. It is safe
to run twice. A leftover
.B onionfruitux
firewall table is deleted, and a DNS snapshot from an older version is
restored. VPN connections are not changed.
.TP
.B reset\-network
Set the system proxy mode to none and remove OnionFruitux's new\-session
environment file. This does not change DNS or VPN connections.
.TP
.B status
Print whether the switch is on, and the active route.
.TP
.B new\-circuit
Ask Tor for a fresh path. The switch must already be on.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that Tor, the
.B onionfruitux
user, GeoIP data, and any selected bridge transport are present.
.B doctor
does not change the network or start Tor.
.TP
.B plan
Print the torrc and the system proxy settings. This command changes nothing.
.TP
.B boot enable
Save the current settings and start Tor at the next boot. It does not set the
system proxy and does not turn the switch on now. The desktop session sets the
proxy when the switch is turned on.
.TP
.B boot disable
Do not turn routing on at startup. It does not turn off a switch that is
already on.
.SH SETTINGS
Open
.B Settings
in the window.
.TP
.B Routes
.B New route
asks for a name, an entry country, and an exit country. Sites see the exit
country. Choose
.B "Only use the countries I picked"
when a circuit that ignores those countries is not acceptable. A route can
also remember a bridge.
.TP
.B Bridges
Optional. Plain, obfs4, Snowflake, meek, WebTunnel, and Conjure. Snowflake,
meek, and obfs4 can use the published default bridges shipped in
.IR onionfruitux/data/pt_config.json .
Paste lines from
.I https://bridges.torproject.org
when a network blocks the defaults, or for plain, WebTunnel, and Conjure.
.TP
.B Network
List ignore hosts (names and networks that stay on the normal connection),
list relay ports (for example
.IR "80, 443" )
when a firewall only allows those outbound ports, and reset the system proxy
mode to none. Relay ports are written as Tor's
.B FascistFirewall
and
.B ReachableAddresses
settings. The page also explains which apps follow the system proxy.
.SH INSTALL
Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint, Pop!_OS, Fedora, Arch, Manjaro, and openSUSE:

.EX
git clone https://github.com/nitrofireinc-pixel/OnionFruitux.git
cd OnionFruitux
sudo ./install.sh
.EE

The installer uses apt, dnf, pacman, or zypper. It installs Tor, nftables, a
bridge transport when the distribution has one (lyrebird, obfs4proxy, or
snowflake\-client), and the Qt libraries used by the window. It creates the
system user
.BR onionfruitux .
It installs this manual to
.I /usr/share/man/man1/onionfruitux.1.gz
and refreshes the manual index with
.B mandb
when that program is present.
.PP
Then pin OnionFruitux to the dock or panel, or run
.B onionfruitux
or
.BR "onionfruitux tray" .
.SH UNINSTALL
.EX
sudo ./uninstall.sh
.EE
removes the program, this manual, the firewall table, and OnionFruitux's Tor
process. The
.B tor
and
.B nftables
packages stay installed.
.EX
sudo ./uninstall.sh \-\-purge
.EE
also removes the
.B onionfruitux
user and
.IR /var/lib/onionfruitux .
.SH RECOVERY
If the computer has no working internet after the app was closed, restore the
saved proxy settings or set the proxy mode to none:
.EX
onionfruitux reset\-network
.EE
The snapshot file is
.IR ~/.local/state/onionfruitux/saved\-state.json .
Opening the window also offers to restore that file.
.PP
Older versions installed an nftables table and redirected DNS. Delete only
that table if it is still present. These commands do not change a VPN:
.EX
sudo nft list table inet onionfruitux
sudo nft delete table inet onionfruitux
onionfruitux panic\-off
.EE
.B panic\-off
stops OnionFruitux's Tor and does not open a browser. Then, if a Tor
process was started with OnionFruitux's torrc and is still running, stop that
process only:
.EX
ps \-eo pid,args | grep /var/lib/onionfruitux/torrc
sudo kill PID
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/onionfruitux/config.json
Routes, bridge lines, and network settings for the current user.
.I XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is used when it is set.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/torrc
The torrc written when the switch turns on.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/tor/
Tor's data directory, owned by the
.B onionfruitux
user.
.TP
.I /var/lib/onionfruitux/state.json
Whether Tor is running. The desktop switch is on only when Tor is running and
the proxy snapshot below exists.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/onionfruitux/saved\-state.json
Proxy settings saved before the switch turned on.
.I XDG_STATE_HOME
is used when it is set.
.TP
.I /etc/onionfruitux/boot.json
Settings saved by
.BR "boot enable" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/onionfruitux.service
Startup unit. It is installed disabled.
.B boot enable
enables it and does not start it.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/onionfruitux.1.gz
This manual.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit\-1/actions/com.nitrofire.onionfruitux.policy
Administrator password prompt for starting and stopping Tor.
.TP
.I /usr/share/tor/geoip
Country database from the
.B tor
package. Country selection needs it.
.TP
.I onionfruitux/data/pt_config.json
Published default bridges.
.I onionfruitux/data/PT_CONFIG_SOURCE.txt
records the Tor Project file they were copied from.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR nft (8)
